EXT. TRAILHEAD - MORNING

MAYA OKONKWO, 30s, laces up her boots at the trailhead sign. Her friend JULES ABARA, 30s, shoulders a daypack.

MAYA
Eight miles round trip, back by six for dinner reservations. Should be an easy day.

JULES
Easy day. I brought extra water just in case anyway.

MAYA
Good call. Let's go.

EXT. TRAIL FORK - MORNING

The trail splits, one path marked with a faded sign, the other unmarked and slightly wider-looking.

JULES
Map says left at the fork. This unmarked one looks more like an actual trail, though.

MAYA
The map's usually right. But sure, this one does look more worn in.

They take the unmarked path instead, the correct one fading behind them.

EXT. TRAIL - MIDDAY

Maya checks the paper map against the trail ahead, frowning slightly.

MAYA
This doesn't totally match what the map's showing. But I think we're still fine.

JULES
We're probably fine. Trails always look a little different in person anyway.

They keep walking without changing course.

EXT. TRAIL - MIDDAY

They stop at a fallen log to drink water.

JULES
Water break. My feet are already a little tired, not going to lie.

MAYA
Mine too, honestly. We're probably about halfway by now, though.

JULES
Probably. Feels about halfway.

They shoulder their packs again and keep going.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

A deer crosses the trail ahead of them and disappears into the brush.

MAYA
Whoa, a deer. That's a good sign, right?

JULES
I don't know if it's a sign of anything. But it was cool to see.

MAYA
Yeah. Cool to see.

They keep walking.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

Maya stops again to check the map, turning it sideways.

MAYA
I really think we should have hit that overlook by now if we were on the right trail.

JULES
Maybe it's just a little further than the map makes it look. Let's give it a bit more.

MAYA
Yeah, let's give it a bit more.

They continue on the same path.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

Another rest stop, another log, nearly identical to the last one.

JULES
Water break again. My feet are really starting to hurt now.

MAYA
Mine too. We should probably almost be there by now.

JULES
Probably almost.

They keep walking without resolving anything.

EXT. TRAIL - LATE AFTERNOON

Maya stops, looking back the way they came.

MAYA
Should we maybe just turn around and go back to the fork? Just to be safe?

JULES
We've come this far already. Feels like a waste to turn back now.

MAYA
Yeah, that's true. Let's just keep going a little more.

They keep going a little more.

EXT. TRAIL - LATE AFTERNOON

Jules stops short, pointing at a weathered wooden post half-buried in brush.

JULES
Wait — that's a trail marker. An actual numbered one. We're on a real trail, just not the one we meant to be on.

MAYA
At least it's a real trail. Let's follow it and see where it connects.

EXT. TRAIL - EVENING

The path widens and joins a familiar-looking junction, the original trailhead sign visible in the distance.

MAYA
There. That's the fork from this morning. We basically looped around the long way.

JULES
The long way, but the right way, eventually. I'll take it.

EXT. TRAILHEAD PARKING LOT - EVENING

Maya and Jules reach the car, dropping their packs against the bumper, tired but relieved.

MAYA
We're going to be late for the reservation, but we made it back in one piece.

JULES
One piece and a much longer hike than we planned. I'll take that trade.

They climb into the car and pull out of the lot as the sun starts to go down.
